            <div xml:id="d301">

                <pb facs="0473" n="371" sameAs="frus:frus1908/pg371"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 9254/4.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Tower to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1908-01-30"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">January 30, 1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1289.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your dispatch No. 763 of the 11th of January, 1908,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d301-f1"/> in which you inclosed to me a copy of a letter from Mr.
                    Joseph Mayer, dated Ephrata, Douglas County, Wash., the 31st of December, 1907,
                    inquiring as to the status of his military case, so called.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to report that in accordance with the instructions contained in
                    Mr. Adee’s dispatch No. 725 of the 14th of October, 1907, a note was addressed
                    to the imperial German ministry for foreign affairs on the 1st of November,
                    1907, submitting the case of Mr. Mayer, and requesting that his American
                    citizenship be recognized, his name stricken from the German military lists, any
                    fine that may be outstanding against him for alleged evasion of military service
                    in Germany dismissed, and that he be permitted to visit his former home, as he
                    desires. No reply to this note has yet been received by me, the time required by
                    the German Government for the investigation and decision of military cases
                    being, on an average, four months. I shall not fail to communicate to you the
                    decision of the German Government in this matter as soon as it is received by
                    me.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charlemagne
                    Tower.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
